>	Wot, no smilies Megan?

>Who gets to say what a simulator is simulating? If someone actually has a
>genuine, licenced THING, surely they are entiltled to use their right-of-use
>of software on a THING-simulator?	;-)

Nope, no smilies, it is a valid question.  Most software is for use
on a single computer system.  Well, what's the difference between a
processor implemented in microcode in a chip and a processor implemented 
in code in memory of something else.  Keep in mind that the emulated 
machine has its own apparent memory, clock, processor, console, disks ..

In fact, the software can identify exactly the type of machine the 
emulator emulates, so how is it to know if it is running on a 'true' 
machine or an emulated one?

The license usually says something like 'for use on the machine on which
it is originally installed ... backup copies may be generated'

I'm sure lawyers could have a field day with this one... :-(

					Megan Gentry
					Former RT-11 Developer



