>connectors, one with 10 the other with 16 pins. I have never seen this
>before.
>Oh, and the drive is marked as beeing a type CM 201 and has a
>manufacturing date of July 1989.

What you have is not a SCSI CDROM.  It's some non-SCSI interface (don't know if
it's private to LMSI or some other not-too-well-known public interface).  But,
the bottom line is that it's not SCSI.  

In the 3100/40 was a board that the internal SCSI connected to, and there was a
ribbon cable, about an inch wide, that ran from that board to the RRD40.  That
board is a SCSI to <whatever> interface board.  You need that board, the ribbon
cable, and some means plugging in your SCSI connector to the SCSI connector on
the interface board.

Let me guess, you want to put this in a PC?

The drive is single-speed, not multi-session, uses the not-current caddy,
doesn't play music, etc., etc.  See if you can sell it and get a new CD that'll
do what you want.

