>stuff). All are running SunOS 4.1, but soon all of them will be put Solaris 2.
>We have credits to buy some new equipment and are hesitating between
>a SparcStation 20.xxx (2 processors) and a DEC Alpha which should be running 
>much faster than the sparc.

>So we're having the following dilemma :

>1. Buying a Sparstation 20 and having no problems in itegrating it in our
>   current configuration. However, we're heavily consuming CPU-time, and 
>   there is a high probability we'll need something faster eventually..

>2. Buying an Alpha, with the risk of having portability problems and other
>   horrible thing happening to our network ;-)

>So what I'd like to know is if any of you have any experience is this kind of
>multi-platform configurations, and if you can help me out of this one.

Something you may not have considered about Solaris 2.x is the overhead of 
learning and maintaining NIS+.  Be aware that pure NIS does not ship with the 
Solaris 2.3 OS.  We have experienced severe problems with NIS+ running in YP 
compatibility mode to service NIS clients (which DEC boxes would be).  The 
solution, in my opinion, is to buy NIS for Solaris 2.3.  It's called the NIS 
Transition Kit, part # NIS-PSITE, I believe, and costs approx. $190 CDN.  I 
have encountered no problems with this package and although I've heard rumours 
of some bugs, they couldn't possibly approach the nightmares with NIS+ we've 
had.  Every time we call Sun for NIS+ assistance, the SE's groan and wince.  

Jeff Richmond


