In article <mschneider.pad.12.0007FFF1@sni.de>, mschneider.pad@sni.de (Marcus Schneider) writes:

>  we have some five VAX systems and thinking about replacing them with UNIX 
> systems. In fact I have absolutely no information about these VAX systems. 
> (Know How died out, only operators left).
>  Has anybody experience in porting software/system to unix ?
> Any kind of information is appreciated..

You have my sincere sympathy.  Maybe you can port the application programs
from VMS to UNIX, it depends upon what VMS-specific language extensions
were used when they were written (uh, you _do_ have current source code
available for the programs, don't you?).  If they were written in a language
for which there is an ANS standard (Fortran or COBOL; if they're old, I
suspect that an ANS-standard C was not available) you have some hope.  If
you're running commercial packaged software, then _maybe_ the vendor has
a UNIX version available -- and maybe not; software companies tend to
go out of business a lot.  Transforming DCL job-control programs into
shell scripts is essentially impossible, as is converting system programs
and assembler programs; these generally need ground-up rewrites.

Our basic problem in doing this has been that there are immense and
unresolveable philosophical differences between VMS and any of the commercial
(proprietary) dialects of UNIX that we have seen.  Both VMS and UNIX have some
immensely valuable ways of doing things that are completely absent in the
other, and if you have programs which depend on one or another of them (such
as keyed access into indexed files in VMS), you have substantial rewriting
in your future.

You may well find that it will be less expensive just to continue use of the
VMS systems, even if you have to hire a consultant to assist you with its
system management part-time. 


