In article <PREP.94Oct26151302@yarrow.wt.uwa.edu.au>, prep@yarrow.wt.uwa.edu.au (Paul Repacholi) writes...
>> 	$ MCR LATCP CREATE LINK FDDI$LINK /DEVICE=FXA0
>> 	$ MCR LATCP SET NODE /STATE=ON
>> 
> 
>Would it not be much better to run LAT on the ethernet, and decnet
>on the FDDI? Can't imagine LAT getting into the 100Mb/s data
>rate...

Not many terminal servers have FDDI adapters too ;-).  I should note that
LAT on VMS can try to use larger packets (~4400 bytes max) when talking FDDI to
FDDI whereas I understand that DECnet phase IV uses 1500 byte message (max).

The only application I could see LAT getting a high data exchange rate would
be for X terminals and like terminal servers, there aren't many X terminals
out there with FDDI adapters...

But there are those who want to run LAT over FDDI and when there are multiple
LAN adapters on the system, it is always a good idea to explicitly create the
link you want for LAT before setting the node state on.  The SET NODE /STATE=ON
command will otherwise create a gratuitous LAT$LINK and you never know which
adapter will be chosen (unless you already create a link or define LAT$DEVICE
appropriately).

