I work in an networked environment of Sun4's, HP 735's, DECstations
(MIPS), SGI's tand DEC Alphas.

A low-end alpha (3000-300LX) that costs $4450 out-performs a more
expensive SparcStation 20-50, based on SPECmark and certainly for
numerical calculations.

The only complication of interoperability with the Sparcs and other
machines is that the Alpha can drive an ethernet into the ground. Any
fast machine can make so much use of the ethernet that other machines
have a hard time competing. Our operations group, a bunch of former
Sun-lovers and come to like OSF/1, particularly when you compare it to
the alternative of using Solaris.

If you need to crank on cycles, do yourself a favour and buy a DEC
2100 using the 275 Mhz CPU's when they're announced in November.

Better yet, ask your local DEC sales critter about the public access
OSF/1 machines (e.g., axposf.pa.dec.com), and try running your program
there to see if you like the machine.


