Folks --

We have a VAXstation 3100 which I would like to take home and use as an X
display for systems at work, as well as running some clients locally.  By the
time it gets home it will be running VMS 6.1.  (I know that uncompressed X over
a phone line isn't going to be very fast, even at 14.4, but I could finesse
this to some extent if I'm running asynch DECnet or NFS over SLIP by using a
local client to act on a remote file.  With SLIP I could run MOSAIC and the
various viewers locally, so most of the screen update stuff wouldn't have to go
over the phone.)

There are a myriad of interesting problems with trying to make this work (since
our terminal servers don't support SLIP and the 3000/300LX we have on order
won't speak asynch DECnet) but the worst one would appear to be that the serial
ports on the 3100 don't have modem control, so I can't use 'em.

I'm not really looking forward to trying to justify buying a terminal server
(and laying the minimum legal distance of Ethernet cable in my apartment) just
to be able to dial in.  (I haven't figured out yet whether I'll also need the
terminal server to drive my LaserJet, so maybe it's "just to be able to dial in
and to print.")

Is there any option cheaper than a terminal server and Ethernet?  Are there
any relatively cheap SCSI terminal ports which VMS will know how to drive? 
(I'm inclined to doubt it.)  Are there serial cards which can go in to the 
bus on the 3100, like the old DZ11 or DMF32?  If so, how much are they and
where can I get them?

Has anybody actually been able to make this work?  If so, what did you do and
how much did it cost?

-- Alan

