In article <1994Oct24.151347.23279@rad.verbex.com>, jtalbott@rad.verbex.com (Jay R. Talbott) writes:
|>We were planning continue along the Exabyte path and get a 2nd tape drive and/
|>or a stacker (such as an 8505XL or a 210, depending upon $$ and whether we go
|>ahead with purchase of the 8 9GB drives), until we got wind of the DEC TZ87,
|>which has higher throughput and more capacity (naturally, at a higher cost).
|>
|>Can anybody out there (outside of DEC, who obviously would be biased) who has
|>experience with the TZ87 tape drive and/or TZ877 stacker give me some feedback
|>on your experiences?  We don't want to waste our money if the "real world"
|>performance of the drive gains us nothing over the Exabyte hardware.
|>
I have a lot of experience with EXB 8500 and EXB10i stacker. I'm rather
happy with the performance but the exabyte hardware and the 8mm tape
need a lot of care and I always keep some drive spare because I break
a couple of drive each year.
The newer generation (8505 and 8505XL) should be more reliable. However
you still need to take a lot of care of your 8mm cassette (use only
Data cartridge, keep them in a constant temperature room)
The DLT is much more reliable and look much solid than the Exabyte stuff
The DLT are twice as fast and I've been able to compress my
binary data 1.7/1
The stacker is also very solid but it contains only 7 cartridges.

You can save some money if you buy a DLTx000 drive from an OEM instead
of a TZxx from DEC but you must contract a good tecnical support.

I consider the DLT tecnology more advanced. DEC (well Quantum) can easily
improve performance with the next generation just by adding more head in
parallel (the dlt2000 has 2 heads --> two serpentines).
Since the drive write a directory in the first part of the tape the drive
can reach directly a file at the beginning of the last track of the serpentine
in few seconds. The average access time is about 45 seconds, with a traditional
sequential tape it can take an hour.
At the end of this year a 20GB (uncomp.) drive should be available and
next year a 40GB drive. But this were plans of DEC I don't know
what Quantum want to do.  Any comment from Quantum?

Exabyte also should have a 20GB drive with a much simper mechanics and so
much less moving parts. But probably this is the limit of the this technology
(in my opinion)
