A number of years ago DEC announced a range of products under the name
DECtalk - a description appeared in DEC's 1985 Terminals & Printers
Handbook.

A DECtalk device acts as an interface between a computer and the
telephone network.  Unlike a modem however, output to the network uses
a voice synthesiser (hence the name), and input is in the form of
"touchtone" (tm) signals which are relayed to the attached computer as
ASCII.

The idea is that you phone a DECtalk machine which answers the phone
and provides a spoken menu.  You can select items in the menu using a
conventional telephone keyboard.


I have an experimental application for which this would be useful.  Please
could anybody tell me whether DECtalk or a low-cost alternative is currently
available.  If somebody has a DECtalk machine that they could spare, even
if only for a short-term loan, then I would be grateful if they were to
contact me.

	Mark


