In article <1994Oct24.151347.23279@rad.verbex.com>,
Jay R. Talbott <jtalbott@rad.verbex.com> wrote:
>Our company's little unix empire seems to be growing it's hard disk storage
>capacity at an exponential rate.  We currently have well over 50 GB of data
>on line, and we are anticipating adding another 8 (yes, 8) 9 GB drives to
>the empire, bringing our total disk storage well up over 120 GB.
>
>Up until recently, most of this disk has been connected to Sun hardware where
>we have used a single Exabyte 8500C tape drive to do backups, manually changing
>the tape during the backups.  As we have migrated onto DEC AXP platforms
>running OSF/1 (currently V3.0), and have radically increased our disk storage,
>this slow manual backup procedure desparately needs improvement!  Thus we are
>investigating our backup hardware options.
>
>We were planning continue along the Exabyte path and get a 2nd tape drive and/
>or a stacker (such as an 8505XL or a 210, depending upon $$ and whether we go
>ahead with purchase of the 8 9GB drives), until we got wind of the DEC TZ87,
>which has higher throughput and more capacity (naturally, at a higher cost).
>
>Can anybody out there (outside of DEC, who obviously would be biased) who has
>experience with the TZ87 tape drive and/or TZ877 stacker give me some feedback
>on your experiences?  We don't want to waste our money if the "real world"
>performance of the drive gains us nothing over the Exabyte hardware.
>
>Thanks,
>
>Jay

Jay,

I bought two TZ877s about 4 months ago and had a few interesting, but not 
horrible problems.  First, DEC shipped the wrong mounting hardware for 
the cabinet.  Second, I ordered them with two HSJ30s, one of which came 
without either cooling fan -- I burned up the HSJ30 the first night it 
was left on.  Third: DEC shipped the wrong redundant power supply (it 
looked like it was for European power).  Fourth: one TZ877 broke right 
away and I had to get DEC to fix it -- it may have been a bad tape 
(that's what DEC said anyway...) but the drives work well now.  I have 
not benchmarked them but they are a h*ll of a lot faster than our 8mm 
subsystems we used to back our disk up to (we used to back-up 10 Gbytes 
in 24 hours, now I do 20 Gbytes in 17 hours).  I'm glad I bought them but 
to speak to the problems I had, my sales person (a VAR, not DEC) said he 
heard rumors of sabotage within DEC's packers and shippers because of all 
the layoffs lately.  Easy to believe since DEC has never screwed up an 
order like that for me before.  

One thing to check is to see how OSF/1 supports you mounting specific 
volumes of the magazine.  Pre V6.1 of VMS doesn't support loading a 
specific volume of the magazine, you have to manually select the tape on 
the front of the tape drive and press *load*, then mount the tape 
yourself.  When that volume spills over, it automatically mounts the next 
tape until it runs out of tapes in the magazine.  Also, see if OSF/1 
allows you to specify tape density through command-line interface -- same 
issue on VMS: can't do it until V6.2.

Other questions, send me Mail

Carl Follstad




