In article <38ieqt$doh@hk.super.net>, lijewski@HK.Super.NET (Mr Mike James Lijewski) writes...
> 
>The following script illustrates an attempt to understand the
>performance (or lack thereof) of an EZ51R Solid State Disk (SSD).

    Arghh. This is a delicate one to respond to, not knowing exactly
    what was sold for what reasons. I'll try a common sense answer, but 
    please remember, this is just a random reader speaking in private time.

>According to the DEC documentation on the disk the maximum throughput
>is 800 I/Os per second.  The best I get in my tests is about 250 I/Os
>per second from one process doing random writes.  I consider this too
>far off the expected maximum to be acceptable. 

    Well, 250 I/Os is not too shabby at all.

    The 800 would be for 1 sector transfers and knee deep queues
    from testbed doing no usefull work other then beating up the
    disk as fast as it can. Ok, so I exagerate a little but you
    get the picture. 

> This was run on a
>four processor DEC Model 2100/500 under OSF/1 V3.0.  Would someone
>with access to an SSD please repeat my experiment.  Either something
>is seriously wrong with my setup or I've been sold a bill of goods.

    There is something sub-optimal the setup it seems. 
    It's the single thread + FSYNC usage. best I know, this means
    you are asking the system to perform each write completely 
    before starting the next. All the latencies are on the critical 
    path, adding to the total delay.
    So while the CPU is preparing the IO, nothing else is happening.
    While the disk is accepting the data, nothing else is happening
    While the drive is storing the data, nothing else is happening.
    While the drive reports success back to the OS, nothing else happens.
    This is assuming a 'dumb' controller. Throw in an HSJ and an other
    level oflatency is introduces.
    So now you found a round trip time of 3.9 mS. Not too bad!

    If this benchmark corresponds _exactly_ with the target customer
    application, then there is nothing much more to do. But supposedly,
    the real appliction has other IOs too, perhaps concurrent processes
    requestion concurrent work to the same drive. If so, the real picture
    might look a whole lot brighter. Then again...

    Also, there is little or nothing to do for 1 CPU, let alone 4.
    Perhaps you can re-run the experiment with 4 concurrent copies
    of the test program each against their won file? The random 
    write loopcount should perhaps be tenfolded or so, to minimize
    the effects of all 4 tests not starting exactly at the same time.
    My guess is that you will come pretty close to 500 IO/s, maybe more.

