In article <jjenkin.10.00E8BA2D@relay.nswc.navy.mil>,
jjenkin@relay.nswc.navy.mil (Jay Jenkins) writes:

|>I am trying to find an alternative to 6250 magtape for backups.  Am currently
|>using aprx. 20-30 reels a night across 2 clusters.  Would like to plug into
|>existing HSC-70 tape cards.  Was looking for 8mm drives, but looks like DEC 
|>has driven out most/all of the competition.  Any opinions on what 
|>would be a cost-effective alternative to the mag-tape, taking into account 
|>the always limited funds?  Sources of reconditioned equipment would also 
|>help.    TIA.

  Assuming thirty 6250 BPI magtapes, each holding (roughly) 140 MB,
  you're looking at a nightly total of about 4.2 GB of backup data.

  If you need HSC connectivity, you are limited to something like the
  TA8xx series.  Depending on the particular hardware at the site and
  on total capacity requirements, you might be better served by a pair
  of Tx8x or TLZ06 drives, or one of the Tx8xx or TLZ6L 'loaders.

  There are likely various third-party solutions available, as well.

  Any of the above can solve your requirements.  Which model best fits
  your needs depends the total capacity desired/required, the budget,
  and on the availability of a connection to a SDI, DSSI or SCSI bus.
  (And on what other systems you plan to exchange data with, if any.)

  And obviously, you should also consider if you have an appropriate
  backup strategy -- the 4.2 GB figure might include more or less data
  than you really need to have backed up per evening.  Incremental vs
  full, files marked nobackup vs backup, too-frequent backups of
  seldom-modified data, etc...


