Jon Kreisler (jon@gdstech.grumman.com) wrote:
: I am about to take the plunge, converting from VAX C v3.2 to DEC C v4.0 on a 
: VAX OpenVMS v5.5-2 system.  I am aware of the problem of being unable to take
: an image created by DEC C and running it on a system only running the older
: libraries.
: I would like to know if anyone has had unpleasant experiences doing this 
: conversion or if there are any other potential "headaches" to be wary of.

I have done this as part of porting stuff to Alpha AXP systems.  For the
most part, things go pretty nicely.  Here is what I had to watch for:

1) start with /STANDARD=VAXC on DEC C.  DEC C type checking is much better
   and stricter than VAX C, so it will yell if you were sloppy and VAX C
   let you get away with it.  Get your code ported, then change /STANDARD=
   to tighten things up.

2) If you have any non-portable code protected by things like #ifdef VAX,
   #ifdef VAXC, #ifdef VMS, etc., make sure you adjust these to do the
   right things in the environment in which you are running DEC C.

Otherwise, all the changes were goodness.

