Number4375 (number4375@aol.com) wrote:
: Don't think me ignorant, but what is Open VMS and VMS?
: I've seen some things about them before and never realy asked the quetion.

VMS is a very high end operating system.  It allows many computers to be
connected together (a little like NFS), it allows multiple processors to
work as one system.  It has enhanced reliability and security features.
It has many things that are add-ons on other systems.  I hardly know
where to start to describe the sophistication of VMS.  It is also large.
We are now running it on 64MB and 128 MB AXP processors.  That is just
enough!  You will need a disk with at least 1 GB of storage to hold the
system and get any work done.  Forget QIC tapes -- you'll fill a drawer
with one backup.  But anyway, it won't work.  The AXP is not a PC.
Therefore, unless DEC or the board vendor has written a device driver
for the board on the AXP, it won't work.  The BIOS extension EPROM
ot the board won't do any good either, because it is x86 code, not
AXP code.  Therefore, only boards (VGA, sound, tape backup, etc.)
that are explicitly supported by open-VMS on the AXP will work.
You'll find open VMS to be a bit pricier than Windows NT, and the shrink-
wrap software at the store won't work either.  DEC's prices are keyed
to CPU model, so there is a different price for each tier of machines.
OpenVMS may not even be available for machines in the desktop 'super PC'
line, or openVMS may be delayed quite a bit, while NT may come out
immediately with the machine.  I happen to like VMS, but I can't afford
to buy 3rd party software for it, so I have a PC, too.  But for real-time
data collection, and writing software as well, I prefer VMS.

					Jon



