>Jeff Lanka
>VMS/Unix SM sysdev
>Dow Jones/Telerate


You mentioned that these problems do not exist in the QA and Production 
systems.  This is important to remember so check the following :

1.  If we assume these 3 development systems are in the same general area, is
    it possible that the power to the machines is bad ?  I would put a power
    line disturbance monitor on the AC line to make sure that the voltage and
    frequency (120VAC, 60 HZ) is not suspect in this area.  Or simply take one
    of the systems from QA or Production and swap it with one in Development 
    and see what happens.    

2.  Were the 3 systems all manufactured at the same time ?  Check the serial
    numbers and date of manufacture.  Ask DEC if other systems from the same
    production batch at different customers are having similar problems.
    If the systems from QA and Production were produced at the same time as 
    these 3 systems then I doubt that you have a manufacturing problem.
    
3.  Are there any configuration or software differences between the machines
    in Development and the others in QA and Production ?   Isolate the systems
    to the lowest common denominator in terms of software and hardware.

4.  Swap a power supply from a known good system in QA or Production with one
    in a Development system.  What happens ?  Do the same with the I/O card,
    motherboard, etc.  By the process of elimination you will find if anything
    is faulty.

5.  Getting 10 power supplies, 10 I/O cards etc replaced in 3 machines does not
    sound reassuring.  Maybe you should demand 3 brand new machines if they 
    can't find the problem. 

Good Luck.


