                  DECUS '94/ANAHEIM - DECEMBER 10-15, 1994
            "Solutions For Reducing The Cost And Risk Of Building
              And Operating Multivendor, Multiplatform Systems"


SHREWSBURY, MA. October 11, 1994 -- Information Technology professionals 
grappling with change in the Digital marketplace and the IT industry as a 
whole, will find the practical real-world solutions they need to succeed 
during DECUS '94/Anaheim - December 10-15,1994 at the Anaheim Convention 
Center, Anaheim, California.

This unique seminars, symposium, and trade show event will put today's IT 
professionals in face-to-face contact with the most valuable resources 
available, allowing them to build relationships that will take them and their 
organizations into the next century.

With a focus on how to successfully build and operate multivendor, 
multiplatform computing environments, DECUS '94/Anaheim will provide 
attendees with state-of-the-practice solutions through a mix of offerings 
presented by industry leaders, product engineers, and developers. Many 
presentations also feature the unbiased and comprehensive insight of 
day-to-day product users.

DECUS '94/Anaheim is the link to the evolving Digital marketplace. This is 
the event where Digital technical and management personnel will share the 
Corporation's technology and product strategy, and explain the new role of 
the sales and distribution channels. It's also the event where attendees can 
ask questions of, and provide candid feedback to, Digital's top level 
managers and vice presidents in the DECUS "Digital Listens Panel".

Highlighted tracks of DECUS '94/Anaheim and the topic areas they will address 
include:

Applications: Architectures and Frameworks; Client/Server and Production 
Systems; Messaging, Groupware, and Workflow

Networks: Networks Protocols and Architectures; Network Operating Systems

Platforms: OpenVMS; UNIX and DEC OSF/1; Windows NT; Cross Platform Services

Database Management Systems: Tuning and Performance; RDBMS Advanced Features; 
High Availability, High Reliability Architectures; Databases for Objects

Operations Management: Disaster Recovery; Organizing the Help Desk; 
Documentation

Special tracks covering Data Warehousing, the Internet, and a UNIX 
Developer's Conference are all part of the DECUS '94/Anaheim program.

The business aspects of today's IT industry including: software licensing; 
the ins and outs of successful outsourcing; managing software contractors; 
and management for IS professionals will also be covered.

The DECUS Trade Show, scheduled for December 12-14, 1994 will feature a 
variety of today's top solutions providers including: Digital; Magic 
Software, Inc.; Lotus Development Corporation; Oracle Corporation; SAS 
Institute, Inc.; WRQ; WordPerfect Corporation; Forest Computer, Inc.; CMD 
Technology, Inc.; and BrainTree Technology, Inc. to name just a few.

Attendees will realize a substantial savings over on-site fees by registering 
for DECUS '94/Anaheim by November 16, 1994. For more information, or to 
register, contact DECUS Customer Service at 1-800-DECUS55.

More information on DECUS '94/Anaheim is also available via: World Wide Web: 
http//www.decus.org/, Gopher: gopher.decus.org, Anonymous FTP: ftp.decus.org, 
E-mail: mailserv@decus.org (send message HELP), TELNET: decus.org, or via 
modem to 508-842-1930 (enter DECUSPROGRAM no password) - provides an 
interactive search-and-query facility.

The U.S. Chapter of DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society, is 
an association of Information Technology professionals interested in the 
products, services, and technologies of Digital Equipment Corporation and 
related vendors. The Association's purpose is to promote the unimpeded 
exchange of information, with the goal of helping its members and their 
organizations to be more successful. The Chapter provides each member with 
means to enhance their professional development, forums for technical 
training, mechanisms for obtaining up-to-date information, advocacy programs, 
and opportunities for informal disclosure and interaction with professional 
colleagues of like interests.

