In article <37kbcv$1kl@jac.zko.dec.com>, lionel@quark.enet.dec.com (Steve Lionel) writes:

>The chip (and the architecture) is endian-neutral, but all of Digital's
>systems which use it are little-endian.  If you wanted to build a
>big-endian system with the Alpha AXP chips you could.

Steve's statement is not (yet) quite correct.  The Alpha AXP architecture,
as currently published, only defines a little-endian data layout.  However,
the 21064 and 21164 implementations of the architecture include extensions
that also allow big-endian data layouts.  As a result, the chips are
endian-neutral.  Indeed, Cray Research uses Alpha AXP chips in big-endian
mode.  It is quite likely that a future update of the Alpha AXP architecture
will include the endian-neutral features of the 21064 and 21164 making
the architecture also be endian-neutral.


