In article <1994Oct12.091737.29623@cc.usu.edu>,  <ivie@cc.usu.edu> wrote:
> So, you're saying that while NT is "open" because it runs on several 
> architectures, it's not "open" enough to run on several different bus
> structures?

"Open" doesn't mean "runs on a variety of platforms".

"Open" means "uses and exposes freely available and documented interfaces".
That can mean "standardized", it can mean "anyone can get the source", it
can mean "available from a variety of sources".

Given Microsoft's penchant for using undocumented internal calls for the
benefit of their own application writers, I'm inclined to discount any
claim that NT is "open". VMS is more "open" than NT.
