In article <414@snm.snm.com>, jeff@snm.com (Jeff Iverson) writes:
<reformatted for width -srh>
|>The subject says it all.  I am in the process of configuring a 3 node
|>DSSI cluster.  I have done this once before but was not able to remove
|>my documentation from the site.  I am trying to recall the mess of
|>cabling issues I had at the time.  
|>
|>If anyone is supporting this configuration or had recent experience
|>I could use any info you might have. The OS will be VMS 5.5-2 on two
|>nodes and 5.5-2H4 on the third node.

   Assuming the operating system versions are compatible (and these
   are -- though I would prefer if you were running OpenVMS V6.1),
   then the other major issue involves the specific type(s) of DSSI
   interface in use.  Not all DSSI adapters can be "middle" nodes.

   In general, these configurations should involve the following:

      o setting the DSSI addresses to a unique value on each DSSI
        host (often 6, 5, and 4) via console command,

      o establishing that the DSSI storage is at unique addresses,
        ( >>>SET HOST/DUP/DSSI/bus:<bus-no> <dssi-no> PARAMS)

      o stringing together the cables, and plugging in the DSSI
        terminators (if terminators required).

   In general, I would recommend that all MSCP storage located within
   a VMScluster be set to unique unit numbers.

   Without information on specific DSSI adapter types and storage
   configuration, it's difficult to go into further detail.  I have
   ignored the software requirements, as these differ little from
   those of other cluster configurations.

   "Triangular" DSSI configurations are also possible -- when one is
   working with three dual-DSSI systems.

   You can (and should) also ask the local Digital office or the
   folks at 1-800-DIGITAL for a copy of the _DSSI VMScluster
   Installation and Troubleshooting Manual_ (EK-410AB-MG).


