|>While compiling some code for debugging purposes & setting the optimization
|>level to 0, I discovered the following anomaly, making -O0 useless for my 
|>debugging:
|>
|>#include <stdio.h>
|>
|>int main(void)
|>{
|>double d;
|>double *dp;
|>unsigned int left = 2;
|>unsigned int order = 3;
|>
|>	dp = &d;
|>
|>	printf("%p\n",dp;
|>	dp += left - order + 1;
|>	printf("%p\n",dp);
|>
|>	return 0;
|>}
|>
|>Under cc -migrate -O1 the program prints out:
|>11ffffaa8
|>11ffffaa8
|>
|>Under cc -migrate -O0 the program prints out:
|>11ffffaa8
|>21ffffaa8
|>
|>Not exactly what I expected under O0, even if subtracting unsigned ints
|>in that manner is not "good programming practice".
|>
|>---------------------
|>Mark Charette
|>MIKA Systems
|>17197 N. Laurel Park Dr.
|>Suite 115
|>Livonia, MI 48152
|>
|>
|>

I recently saw a similar discussion elsewhere that boiled down
to "negative unsigned ints" being defined in the C standard
as implementation defined.  What is the value of 2u - 3u? 

This may be a real pain, but change your types to int if
they can be negative or change the expression to 
(left + 1) - order which will have well defined behavior
in all compilers for left = 2u, order = 3u.

