In article <374qn0$rfn@sefl.satelnet.org> martinson@satelnet.org (Yanek Martinson) writes:
>Any numbers, estimates, opinions, etc., on the performance of LAT
>when compared to telnet or rlogin?

Kind of hard to respond without more detail as to environment and usage.

LAT was designed as a protocol to be used over a local Ethernet, and is not
routable as a network level protocol and must be bridged.

LAT seems to work OK in it's intended environment (ignoring the fact that
it is a proprietary protocol).

LAT tends to be fragile when bridged over WANs and will often lose the
terminal session (logging out the user).  Being TCP/IP based, telnet and
rlogin work reliably over a much wider range of networks and conditions.

Art



