In article <CxBHq7.KJH@murdoch.acc.Virginia.EDU> rmh2a@fulton.seas.Virginia.EDU (Cucumber) writes:
>I am a student researching the architecture of the DEC Alpha, specifically
>the instruction set, register set, and other programmer-visible influences
>of its pipelined implementation.  Does anyone have any information or know
>where information can be obtained?  Thanks in advance!

It's important to distinguish between the Alpha architecture, and the
various implementations available now and in the future.  The architecture
mostly specifies what features a programmer can rely upon to produce
a correctly-working program.  Features specific to a given implementation
may affect performance; for example, the right way to schedule instructions
to keep the pipeline happy.

There's a lot of information on our main Web server, "http://www.digital.com".
For example, in "http://www.digital.com/info/document.dir.html" I found

DECchip 21064-AA Microprocessor: Hardware Reference Manual
   Product Documentation -- Abstract , PostScript 
   October 1992, 316 Pages

DECchip 21064/21064A Alpha AXP Microprocessors: Hardware Reference Manual
   Product Documentation -- Abstract , PostScript 
   June 1994, 384 Pages

There's also an issue of the Digital Technical Journal online with
various Alpha papers, and there's the "Alpha Architecture Reference
Manual" (Richard Sites, Digital Press) which might or might not be
available online (sorry, I don't know).

-Jeff


