In article <37eanj$dk1@lll-winken.llnl.gov>,  <oberman@icaen.llnl.gov> wrote:
>In Article <37cq2q$at9@usenet.rpi.edu>
>wilsonj@alum01.its.rpi.edu (John Wilson) writes:
>>FLAME BAIT!

>PC/MS-DOS diverges quite significantly from RT-11 after the first version,
>rapidly becoming very Unix-like. But if you take a look at V1 and its source
>code, the descent from RT-11 is quite obvious.

Well, no.  RT-11 gives you async I/O (everything is done on interrupts),
uses channel numbers to point to open files, supports the concept of
vector ownership and jam lists, has dynamically installable/uninstallable
device drivers, and optionally gives you multiple job contexts.

V1 of DOS gave you FCBs (i.e. keeping track of open file info is the
user program's responsibility), polled I/O, and you were (and are) totally
on your own if you want to program devices directly without crashing the
system on a ^C (if you get the ^C at all, since DOS only polls for them
on DOS calls).  The OS was (is) no more than a set of subroutines you
can call, unless PRINT.COM is running DOS never gets control of the
machine at all unless you call it.

But, the outer veneer looks familiar -- 3-letter filename extensions,
device names with a colon, ^C aborting a program (maybe, if you're lucky),
^S/^Q flow control, and commands with names like DIR, TYPE, REN (as in
RT V4 and later), are enough to fool the casual user into thinking there's
some similarity.  But it goes no deeper than that.

John Wilson
Current user of RT-11 V4, *under* PC-DOS V6.1


