oberman@icaen.llnl.gov writes:

>For anyone serious about OS design, it has a great deal worth reading and is
>NOT a humorous treatment although many of the stories are funny as long as they
>happen to someone else. Just as disturbing is that a large number of the
>problems pointed out in Unix are also appropriate to MS-DOS. That covers the
>vast majority of all existing computer systems. Considering that PC-DOS V1 was
>a pretty direct port of DEC's RT11, you can really see how the Unix way has
>taken over in other places.

  The "UNIX-hater's Handbook" is a book which is ungraciously critical
of UNIX - the the point of making the *authors* look rather stupid.  I
have an *extreme* dislike of UNIX and there are sections of the book
where I am in complete agreement.  However, some sections of the book
harp on how UNIX worried about backward compatibility while others
harp on how UNIX *didn't* worry about backward compatibility.  The
writing is needlessly bitter and I think gives something of a bad name
to the rest of us UNIX-haters.

  I wish that a book would come out called "Debunking UNIX" or "Snake
Oil" which uses the same approach of excerpting actual cases with
light reading to get the meat of the UNIX-hating mindset across -
without alienating people who are on the fence.  Mudslinging does not
advance anybody's cause.

  The book should have been about half as thick - and I'd gladly
resell my copy.  It's not a keeper, and I regret turning my money over
to the authors.

  The quotes at the beginning of the chapters are pretty good, though.

JB
