Chuck Mayhall (cmayhall@satland.win.net) wrote:
: I am in need of some info on a MICROVAX II. I've had it for a while and know next to 
: nothing about it. It has the following:

:         1 RQDX# SCSI Disc controller (no drive) (M7555)
:         1. Can I use any SCSI Disc Drive?
[I'm assuming you meant RQDX3]
Now, I'm no DEC hardware expert, but I do have MicroVax II in my basement 
I'm working on reviving.  Anyhow, the M7555 is just an MFM controller, not
SCSI.  Typical DEC drives that were used with this card were the RD53 and
RD54; my MicroVax II had an RD54 in it, which is a Maxtor XT-2190 MFM drive.

:         2. What do I need to be able to hook up a monitor and keyboard or terminal?
You need a basic, dumb text terminal, such as a VT-100.  I just use your PC; take the console cable you [should have] gotten with the thing, and just plug it
into a serial port on your PC or MAC (Mac modem cable needed if you have a Mac)
and then run your favorite com software,
setting it to VT100 or something similar.  You don't even need a null modem
adapter, as DEC takes care of the signals properly in their console cable.

:         3. Is this thing worth fooling with?
I hope so.  I really want mine to work! :-)  It should make a very nice
home workstation, if you get it up and running.  But, unless you get
lucky, you could end up spending more than the thing is worth just getting
hardware, like the hard disk. 

These are just my opinions and knowledge from working with my own mvaxII;
I don't purport to be a DEC expert... :-)

Jonathan
sturges@cs.odu.edu


