The DEC approach is to put the cpu chip on a daughterboard, so that you
can swap a current Intel daughterboard to an Alpha one (available this
month [October], I think).

Alternatively, you can get an Alpha-based sub-system from a UK company
called Myriad Solutions; this fits into a vacant drive slot of a tower
PC, connects to ISA/EISA/PCI bus, and accelerates your PC no end. The
one I saw at a seminar back in March had a 21064 cpu, 64Mb memory,
dedicated 100Mb/s I/O bus, and was engineered into a standard PC hard
drive slot (including its own fan in the front plate). Very neat,
although sadly not very cheap; if anyone is interested in more info the 
company is based in Cambridge in the UK - the only contact data I have 
is their phone number: +44 (0)1223 421181.

