All DECpc XL series machines have the CPU on a daughtercard.  486 and
Pentium cards have been available for some time.  The AXP card was
not announced in the September 1994 PCatalog, so I'm not sure that it
is available now.  The rumor I heard was that the card was done but
was not going to be available until Windows NT 3.5 came out, because
NT 3.1 did not support the card and PCI bus.  Maybe it is available
now (because NT 3.5 is now in production), a call to a 1-800-PCBYDEC
should get an answer.

I believe the daughtercard contains the CPU, clocks, and L2 cache.
I don't know what kind of bus goes off of the card, but the PCI,
ISA, and main memory slots are on the motherboard, not the daughtercard.

Also, the 486 cards have a Pentium OverDrive socket, should Intel ever
let you go that route, or you could change to the Pentium daughtercard.

