You say you have not heard of such a thing.  May I direct your attention to the
DECpc XL line?  The processor is on a plug-in card, so you can swap out a 33Mhz
486dx for a Pentium or an Alpha by removing the old processor card and adding a
new one.  (Digital may recommend that an authorized provider do the swap; I
don't remember.)  The box is a mini-tower with a mixture of PCI and ISA slots -
and support for both 5V and 3.3V power supply voltages.  I think there's also a
version called the XL Server which features a mixture of PCI and EISA.  They
are very nice machines, although personally I favor having a very large number
of front-panel 5.25" drive bays and EISA/ISA slots, and wish the XL had just a
few more of them.  I have read that the 3.3V power supply is a real advantage
for anyone considering a processor upgrade - it lets things like Pentiums run
without the need for heat-generating 5V to 3.3V converters on the motherboard.

To the best of my knowledge, it is true that only the XL and XL Server feature
the ability to take 486, Pentium, or Alpha processors.  So if this salesman is
trying to convince you that a DECpc LPv, LPx, etc. provides this benefit - ask
him or her to show you where we claimed that those models can do that.  There
may also be information on our Web server, so you can pull the advertisements
if you want to see them.

Disclaimer:  I work in a different part of Digital from the PC group - and the
above is what I remember without looking up stuff.  Check with someone whose
job is to provide this information if you want the official low-down.  I just
came back from a conference that was one long experiment in sleep deprivation,
so I may have gotten some of the details wrong.

