   I believe certain models of DEC PCs are upgradeable to take an
AXP motherboard, or have the CPU on a daughtercard which may be replaced
with an AXP daughtercard. There is an AXP based PC already, which comes
in a standard looking DEC PC case, so the technology to do the upgrade is
certainly available today. OTOH I don't know that these upgrades tend to be
cost effective, they are usually relatively expensive and you may very well
find that it's cheaper to sell/trade your old system and buy a newer one
when it's time to upgrade ( this is a general principle, not specific to
DEC's upgrade offerings ). 

   You also need to consider that if you upgrade to a non-Intel processor
you may not be able to run all the programs you want, or that they don't
run nearly as fast as they should because of the efficiency of the emulation.
The home software market is firmly entrenched in Intel specific code right
now, and I wouldn't expect much native code for other architectures for quite
some time ( perhaps in 5 years or so if Windows/NT ever takes off ).

> : Also from my meager knowledge of computers. One important aspect as to the  
> : speed of the computer is the bus and bus speed are these PC busses the  
> : same as the Alpha busses if not won't they slow it down a lot??
> 
> Truth is that the alpha busses generally run at ~40Mhz +- 10 Mhz as
> best I can rember; these are about the same speeds as those in a
> typical 486.  
> 

 The real factors affecting speed are the CPU, primary and secondary caches,
and the speed of the main memory. Memory access is done through a private bus,
so the speed of the I/O bus isn't relevant. On most systems today the video is
also on a special bus, leaving just disks, network, printer, modems, etc on the
system bus. The bus speed isn't really all that relevant unless you are doing a
whole lot of I/O, something one doesn't usually see in a home computer. An EISA
bus should have more than enough capacity to handle all of these in a home
computer environment ( unless you're planning to run half a dozen fast SCSI
cards flat out ), in fact I doubt you'd do anything which would saturate an
ISA bus.

