     I didn't see the original post, but if you are trying to do bit 
manipulations in Pascal, there is a very easy (and 100% "legal") way to do 
it, taking advantage of the SET type.

     Suppose you have a 16-bit register, with the bits defined as follows:

Bit 0   Go
Bit 6   Interrupt Enable
Bit 7   Ready flag
Bit 15  Error flag

Use a 16-element enumerated type to name the bits, and make a set out of 
them.

TYPE
  registerbittype = (go, bit1, bit2, bit3, bit4, bit5, intenable, 
			readyflag, bit8, bit9, bit10, bit11, bit12, bit13,
			bit14, errorflag);
  registerset = SET OF registerbittype;

Most Pascal implementations (and you need to check on this!) will implement 
the registerset defined above as a two-byte "array of bits", just what you 
want.  You manipulate the bits by simple set operations.

VAR
 deviceregister : registerset;

PROCEDURE cleardevice

BEGIN   { cleardevice }
 deviceregister := []
END;

PROCEDURE enableinterrupt;

BEGIN   { enableinterrupt }
 deviceregister := deviceregister + [intenable]
END;

PROCEDURE disableinterrupt;

BEGIN   { disableinterrupt }
 deviceregister := deviceregister - [intenable]
END;

FUNCTION oopserror : boolean;

BEGIN   { oopserror }
 oopserror := errorflag IN deviceregister
END;


Note that I'm assuming that you have some way of defining "deviceregister" 
to be either the hardware register itself or an appropriate copy of it.

I've successfully used this technique on both the PC and PDP-11s.  I find 
the use of Pascal sets to represent bits this way quite intuitive and easy 
to understand.  Note that all of the "thinking" goes into the definition of 
the enumerated type -- that's the only place that actual knowledge of the 
hardware is needed (to define the bit locations).

Bob Schor
Pascal/PDP-11 enthusiast



